General elections were held in [[Costa Rica]] on 7 February 2010. The ruling party before the election, the center-left [[National Liberation Party (Costa Rica)|National Liberation Party]], put forward former [[Vice President of Costa Rica|Vice-President]] [[Laura Chinchilla]] as its presidential candidate, while the [[Libertarianism|libertarian]], [[Movimiento Libertario]] nominated former legislator [[Otto Guevara]]. Opinion polls before voting started consistently put Chinchilla as the front-runner, a trend confirmed in the election-night count, which showed her garnering 46.76% of the vote.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.nacion.com/ln_ee/2010/febrero/07/pais2255136.html |title=(in Spanish) |access-date=2010-02-08 |archive-date=2010-02-11 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100211122142/http://www.nacion.com/ln_ee/2010/febrero/07/pais2255136.html |url-status=dead }}</ref>

The election was supervised by observers from several countries, as well as from the [[Organization of American States]].<ref>{{cite web|last=Jara |first=Francisco |url=https://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5gZx0yDwahBg3umqRGIX96uw4E--w |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130124161553/http://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5gZx0yDwahBg3umqRGIX96uw4E--w |url-status=dead |archive-date=24 January 2013 |title=AFP: First female poised for Costa Rica presidency |date=2010-02-06 |access-date=2010-08-21}}</ref> The incumbent president, [[Óscar Arias]], was ineligible to run for a second consecutive term. This was the last time as of 2019, that the National Liberation Party has gotten more than 30% of the vote, the last time to this date that they have won the presidency, and the last time it has won any province in what is known as the Central Valley (the four provinces in the interior of the country: San José, Alajuela, Heredia and Cartago).

==Presidential candidates==

Candidates included:

*[[Laura Chinchilla Miranda]] (ruling [[National Liberation Party (Costa Rica)|National Liberation Party]], vice-president 2006–08)
*[[Ottón Solís]] (oppositional [[Citizens' Action Party (Costa Rica)|Citizens' Action Party]], candidate in 2006)
*[[Otto Guevara]] ([[Movimiento Libertario]], candidate in 2006)
*Luis Fishman (conservative [[Social Christian Unity Party]], vice-president under [[Abel Pacheco]])

Two weeks before the election, Patriotic Alliance and National Integration Party's candidates, Rolando Araya and Walter Muñoz, stopped their campaigns and endorsed Citizens' Action Party's candidate Otton Solís, in an effort to build a progressive alliance against Laura Chinchilla.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.nacion.com/el-pais/politica/araya-y-munoz-renuncian-a-ser-candidatos-y-apoyan-a-solis/DRRTC4TAPBBVBNP2472YW3I66I/story/|title = Araya y Muñoz renuncian a ser candidatos y apoyan a Solís}}</ref>

==Parliamentary elections==
The swifting from a [[two-party system]] to a [[multi-party system]] was much more evident in this election<ref name="IPU">{{cite journal|title=Panorama of Parliament Elections 2010|journal=International Parliamentary Union|url=http://www.ipu.org/pdf/publications/elections10-e.pdf}}</ref><ref name="Lehring">{{cite news|last1=Lehring|first1=Gary|title=Costa Rican legislative elections show growing voter dissatisfaction with traditional choices|url=http://www.ticotimes.net/2014/02/15/costa-rican-legislative-elections-show-growing-voter-dissatisfaction-with-traditional-choices|access-date=13 March 2016|agency=The Tico Times|date=15 February 2014}}</ref><ref name="Landsford">{{cite book|last1=Landsford|first1=Tom|title=Political Handbook of the World 2014|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=iC_VBQAAQBAJ&q=costa+rica%27s+end+of+two+party+system&pg=PA333|access-date=13 March 2016|isbn=9781483386263|date=2014-03-20}}</ref><ref name="Landsford 2">{{cite book|last1=Landsford|first1=Tom|title=Political Handbook of the World 2012|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=d33MfEFXvW8C&q=costa+rica%27s+end+of+two+party+system&pg=PA332|access-date=13 March 2016|isbn=9781608719952|date=2012-04-02}}</ref>

For the then three major parties; [[National Liberation Party (Costa Rica)|PLN]], [[Citizens' Action Party (Costa Rica)|PAC]] and [[Libertarian Movement (Costa Rica)|ML]] the voting for the presidential ballot was superior to the support in the legislative, as for example PLN presidential candidate [[Laura Chinchilla]]<ref name="IPU"/> received 46%<ref name="ER2">{{cite web|title=7 February 2010 Presidential Election Results - Costa Rica Totals|url=http://www.electionresources.org/cr/president.php?election=2010|website=Elections Resources|access-date=13 March 2016}}</ref> of the votes and PLN's legislative ballot only 37%.<ref name="ER">{{cite web|title=7 February 2010 Legislative Assembly Election Results - Costa Rica Totals|url=http://www.electionresources.org/cr/deputies.php?election=2010|website=Election Resources|access-date=13 March 2016}}</ref> Similarly PAC's candidate [[Ottón Solís]] with 25%<ref name="ER2"/> presidential against 17% legislative<ref name="ER"/> and [[Otto Guevara]] with 20%<ref name="ER2"/> oppose to 14% legislative.<ref name="ER"/> Contrary to PUSC whose candidate [[Luis Fishman]] received 3%<ref name="ER2"/> electoral support while his party received 8%.<ref name="ER"/>

This was at the time PAC's worst electoral result in its history having the smallest faction in the Parliament<ref name="Lehring"/> and ML's best result with to this date its biggest.<ref name="Lehring"/> PLN only lost one seat. Left-wing [[Broad Front (Costa Rica)|Broad Front]] maintained its only seat in the person of future presidential nominee [[José María Villalta Florez-Estrada]]<ref name="Lehring"/> and two Christian parties<ref>{{cite news|last1=Lopez|first1=Jaime|title=Civic Groups Move Against Gay Marriage in Costa Rica|url=http://news.co.cr/civic-groups-move-against-gay-marriage-in-costa-rica/23281/|access-date=13 March 2016|agency=Costa Rica Star|date=July 18, 2013}}</ref> for the first time had deputies at the same time; [[Costa Rican Renewal Party]] and its provincial offshoot [[National Restoration Party (Costa Rica)|National Restoration]].<ref name="Lehring"/>

==Opinion polls==
{{Chart|definition=Opinion polling for the 2010 Costa Rican presidential election.chart|data=Opinion polling for the 2010 Costa Rican presidential election.tab|width=720px}}
*''CID-Gallup'' 24 January 2010: '''Chinchilla''' 43%; Guevara 30%; Solís 15%; Fishman 8%
*''Borge & Asociados for Diario Extra opinion poll'' January 2010: '''Chinchilla''' 38.7%; Guevara 18.3%; Solís 9.6%; Fishman 3.7%
*''Demoscopía for Al Día'' January 2010: '''Chinchilla''' 45.1%; Guevara 30.1%; Solís 9.5%; Fishman 3.8%<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.angus-reid.com/polls/view/34985/chinchilla_leads_guevara_gains_in_costa_rica/ |title=Chinchilla Leads, Guevara Gains in Costa Rica: Angus Reid Global Monitor |publisher=Angus-reid.com |access-date=2010-08-21 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100205054502/http://www.angus-reid.com/polls/view/34985/chinchilla_leads_guevara_gains_in_costa_rica |archive-date=2010-02-05 |url-status=usurped }}</ref>
*December 2009: '''Chinchilla''' 36.7%; Guevara 16.2%; Solís 8.5%; Fishman 2.2%
*December 2009: '''Chinchilla''' 46.6%; Guevara 19.5%; Solís 8.3%; Fishman 4.1%
*October 2009: '''Chinchilla''' 53.0%; Guevara 15.7%; Solís 12.3%; Fishman 1.5%

==Results==
===President===
At 9:08&nbsp;p.m. local time on election day, 7 February second-placed candidate Otton Solis conceded defeat to [[Laura Chinchilla]], who will become Costa Rica's first female president. With approximately 40% of the vote counted, Chinchilla was consistently surpassing the 40% threshold for victory in the first round, leading Solis by 47% to 24%, with third-placed candidate Otto Guevara trailing at 21.5%.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.nacion.com/ln_ee/2010/febrero/07/pais2255136.html |title=Solís reconoce victoria de Chinchilla - EL PAÍS |publisher=nacion.com |access-date=2010-08-21 |archive-date=2010-02-11 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100211122142/http://www.nacion.com/ln_ee/2010/febrero/07/pais2255136.html |url-status=dead }}</ref>

